1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,360
There is a star coming towards us.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,600
So how close will it get, and should you
worry?

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,840
This is not a guess and not a rumour.

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:13,180
The star has been measured, and it is
heading almost straight at the sun.

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:17,200
Its name is Gliese 710.

6
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:23,300
In about one point three million years, it
will pass closer to us than any star we

7
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:24,140
know of.

8
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:29,620
It will not hit anything, but it will sail
right through the outer edge of our solar

9
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:30,520
system.

10
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:36,260
In this film, I will show you where that
star is now, how we found out how close it

11
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:41,240
will come, what it will pass through, and
what our night sky will look like when it

12
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:42,100
arrives.

13
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:47,500
Right now, Gliese 710 is about 62
light-years away in the

14
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:50,100
constellation of Serpens, the serpent.

15
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:56,520
It is an orange dwarf, a little smaller
and cooler than the sun, with about 60%

16
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:57,540
of the sun's mass.

17
00:00:58,640 --> 00:00:59,520
You cannot see it.

18
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:03,580
It is far too faint for your eyes on even
the darkest night.

19
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:06,520
You would need binoculars just to find it.

20
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:09,000
So why does it matter?

21
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,640
Because of the way it moves.

22
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:17,720
Most stars drift slowly sideways across
our sky year after year.

23
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:21,920
Gliese 710 hardly drifts at all.

24
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,240
It is coming towards us at about 14
kilometres every second, more than

25
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:33,640
50,000 kilometres an hour, and almost none
of that motion is sideways.

26
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:39,780
When something barely moves across your
view but keeps getting closer, it is

27
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:40,620
heading for you,

28
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:44,580
like a car on a long, straight road at
night.

29
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:47,600
Its headlights do not slide left or right.

30
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:49,200
They just grow.

31
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:55,780
If you saw our film on the nearest star,
you met Proxima Centauri about four

32
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:56,720
light-years away.

33
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:00,200
Proxima is our nearest neighbour today,

34
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:06,040
but stars move, and the title of nearest
does not last forever.

35
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,100
How do we know where it will be in a
million years?

36
00:02:09,639 --> 00:02:11,020
For a long time, we did not.

37
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:13,840
We were not even sure it would come close
at all.

38
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:19,780
In the late 1990s, a satellite called
Hipparcos measured the star's position and

39
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:20,660
motion.

40
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:25,300
The best guess then was that it would pass
about a light-year from the sun, and the

41
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:26,980
uncertainty was huge.

42
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:32,360
Then came Gaia, the European spacecraft
that has measured more than a billion

43
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:33,320
stars.

44
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:39,100
With every new release of its data, the
path came into sharper focus, and the

45
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:41,040
star kept coming closer.

46
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:45,720
The latest Gaia measurements put the
closest approach at about a fifth of a

47
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:46,580
light-year.

48
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:51,920
That is about 10,000 to 13,000 times
further than Earth is from the sun,

49
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,800
five times closer than the old estimate.

50
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,940
That still sounds far, so let's shrink it.

51
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:01,440
Make the gap between the sun and Earth one
centimetre.

52
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:07,580
Neptune, our outermost planet, is then 30
centimetres away, about the length of a

53
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:08,480
ruler.

54
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:13,960
And Gliese 710 passes more than 100 metres
away, the length of a

55
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:14,820
football pitch.

56
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,520
So the star will not come anywhere near
the planets.

57
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:21,480
But what is out there 100 metres from the
sun?

58
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:23,760
Something we have never seen.

59
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:26,500
We make one of these every week.

60
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:29,440
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

61
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:34,400
Far beyond the planets, the sun is wrapped
in a vast cloud of ice.

62
00:03:35,240 --> 00:03:40,640
Trillions of frozen bodies, the leftovers
from when the planets formed, drifting so

63
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:43,680
far out that the sun's pull on them is
barely a whisper.

64
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:49,180
It is called the Oort cloud, and it is
still one of the great unknowns of our

65
00:03:49,240 --> 00:03:50,060
solar system.

66
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:52,840
Nobody has ever seen it directly.

67
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,600
We know it is there because of the comets
that fall out of it on journeys that take

68
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:01,040
them hundreds of thousands of years or
more.

69
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:06,280
Its inner edge is thought to start a few
thousand times further out than Earth.

70
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:09,840
Its outer edge may reach 100,000.

71
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:15,600
Sunlight, which gets to us in eight
minutes, would take months to reach the

72
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:20,740
place where Gliese 710 will pass, and that
is exactly where the star is

73
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:21,600
going,

74
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:24,000
straight through the Oort cloud.

75
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:29,760
A star passing through the cloud is like a
hand brushing through a bowl of floating

76
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:30,660
marbles.

77
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:34,060
Its gravity nudges the comets nearest its
path.

78
00:04:34,440 --> 00:04:36,540
Some get flung out into the galaxy.

79
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:40,580
Others are knocked onto new orbits that
fall in towards the sun.

80
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:47,060
The calculation suggests that Gliese 710
will send about 10 extra comets a year,

81
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:52,320
falling in close enough for us to see, and
it will keep happening for three to four

82
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:53,520
million years.

83
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:56,460
But here is the strange part.

84
00:04:57,100 --> 00:05:00,040
The comets do not arrive while the star is
passing.

85
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:01,660
Why?

86
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:07,080
Because they are so far out that falling
in takes hundreds of thousands of years.

87
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:12,680
By the time the shower is at its height,
Gliese 710 will already be heading away

88
00:05:12,760 --> 00:05:15,460
again, shrinking back into the dark.

89
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:17,620
Should anyone worry?

90
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:19,440
Not much.

91
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:22,640
Most comets that fall in never come close
to Earth.

92
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:27,240
Jupiter and Saturn throw many of them back
out or steer them away.

93
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:33,640
What the people of that far future get is
a sky with more comets in it for a

94
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:35,000
very long time.

95
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:36,640
So what would you see?

96
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:40,360
Imagine standing on Earth on the night of
the closest pass.

97
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:44,100
A new star has been brightening for
thousands of years.

98
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:49,540
Now it is the brightest star in the whole
night sky, about three times brighter than

99
00:05:49,580 --> 00:05:54,400
Sirius and as bright as Jupiter at its
best, and it glows orange.

100
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:58,900
It will not light up the night like the
moon, and it will not warm anyone.

101
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:03,520
It is still billions of times fainter than
our sun, but it will move.

102
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:06,720
Most stars look fixed for a lifetime.

103
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:12,420
This one will creep across the sky by
about the width of the full moon every 35

104
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:13,300
years.

105
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:17,300
Then, slowly, it will fade and drift away
for good.

106
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:20,960
Is this the first time a star has come
this close?

107
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:22,600
Not at all.

108
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:26,780
Gliese 710 will not be the first star to
pass this way.

109
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:33,120
About 70,000 years ago, a dim red pair of
stars, now called Scholz's

110
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:38,360
Star, skimmed through the outer Oort cloud
less than a light-year from the sun.

111
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:42,520
It was too small and too faint to stir up
much.

112
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:48,780
And about 2.8 million years ago, a star
much like our sun, called

113
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:54,400
HD 7977, may have passed as close as
Gliese 710

114
00:06:54,460 --> 00:06:55,260
will.

115
00:06:56,020 --> 00:07:01,300
Gaia's measurements cannot yet pin down
exactly how close, so this is not a rare

116
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:02,980
disaster waiting to happen.

117
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:05,840
It is how life works in a galaxy.

118
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:12,000
The sun is one star among billions, all of
them moving, and every few

119
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:14,780
million years, one of them brushes past.

120
00:07:16,860 --> 00:07:22,080
What if the night sky you have always
known is not fixed at all, but a slow

121
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:24,720
traffic of stars still on the move?

122
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:28,160
The bigger question is the cloud itself.

123
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:34,320
Some astronomers think the sun was born in
a crowd of sister stars, and that many of

124
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:36,980
the comets in our Oort cloud were once
theirs.

125
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:43,080
If they are right, every star that passes
is part of a very old exchange.

126
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:49,340
Some of the ice falling in from the dark
may have started out around another sun.
